Still reproduces on **herdr 0.9.5** (Homebrew, ad-hoc signed), **macOS 27.0.1**, Ghostty as the host terminal (Local Network allowed). The server runs detached (parent is launchd, PID 1).

Same `node` one-liner connecting to a LAN host:

| Binary | Started from | Result |
|---|---|---|
| node (nvm) | herdr pane | `EHOSTUNREACH` |
| node (nvm) | fresh Ghostty window, no herdr | connects |
| Xcode `python3` | herdr pane | `[Errno 65] No route to host` |
| `/usr/bin/curl`, `/usr/bin/nc`, `/usr/bin/perl` | herdr pane | connect |

Apple platform binaries are exempt from Local Network Privacy, and loopback is never filtered. Together they give a workaround that survives server restarts, `live-handoff`, and `herdr update`, with no app bundle and no TCC grant: a small loopback relay running on `/usr/bin/perl`. Clients point at `127.0.0.1:<port>` instead of the LAN address.

<details>
<summary>Relay script (~50 lines, perl core modules only)</summary>

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
# Loopback TCP relay to a LAN host. /usr/bin/perl is a platform binary, so
# Local Network Privacy does not block it; clients connect over loopback.
# Usage: relay.pl <listen_port> <lan_host> <lan_port>
use strict;
use warnings;
use IO::Socket::INET;
use IO::Select;
use POSIX ':sys_wait_h';

my ($listen_port, $host, $port) = @ARGV;
die "usage: $0 listen_port host port\n" unless defined $port;

$SIG{CHLD} = sub { 1 while waitpid(-1, WNOHANG) > 0 };
$SIG{PIPE} = 'IGNORE';

my $server = IO::Socket::INET->new(
    LocalAddr => '127.0.0.1', LocalPort => $listen_port,
    Listen => 64, ReuseAddr => 1, Proto => 'tcp',
) or die "listen 127.0.0.1:$listen_port: $!\n";

while (1) {
    my $client = $server->accept or next;
    my $pid = fork;
    if (!defined $pid) { close $client; next }
    if ($pid) { close $client; next }
    close $server;
    my $up = IO::Socket::INET->new(PeerAddr => $host, PeerPort => $port, Timeout => 10)
        or do { print STDERR "connect $host:$port: $!\n"; exit 1 };
    pump($client, $up);
    exit 0;
}

sub pump {
    my ($a, $b) = @_;
    my %peer = ($a => $b, $b => $a);
    my $sel = IO::Select->new($a, $b);
    my $buf;
    while ($sel->count) {
        for my $fh ($sel->can_read) {
            my $n = sysread($fh, $buf, 65536);
            my $to = $peer{$fh};
            if (!$n) { $sel->remove($fh); shutdown($to, 1); next }
            my $off = 0;
            while ($off < $n) {
                my $w = syswrite($to, $buf, $n - $off, $off);
                return unless defined $w;
                $off += $w;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

</details>

Run it as a user LaunchAgent with `ProgramArguments` = `/usr/bin/perl`, the script path, and `<port> <lan-host> <lan-port>`, plus `RunAtLoad` and `KeepAlive`. Under launchd it reaches the LAN host as well. Verified here with streaming OpenAI-compatible completions from an agent CLI running inside a herdr pane. It only helps for fixed TCP endpoints (an API server and the like), not for mDNS discovery or arbitrary LAN access, so a proper fix in herdr (#5406) is still needed.
